---
title: "Migrate sparse BM25 to server BM25"
description: "Move a client-side sparse BM25 setup to Qdrant 1.20 server BM25: add a BM25 text index on the existing payload field and switch queries — no re-upload."
sidebar:
  order: 3
---

If your collection stores BM25 as a sparse named vector and also keeps the
source text in the payload, you can move to
[server BM25](/docs/guides/bm25-server/) on Qdrant 1.20 without re-uploading
a single point.

{% aside type="tip" title="No re-upload" %}
Server BM25 indexes the payload text you already have. Creating the index is
the whole data migration; existing points and the sparse vector stay as they
are.
{% /aside %}

## Before: client sparse BM25

The client encodes text with the `qdrant/bm25` model in `qql-embed` and
searches a sparse vector:

{% qqlExample title="Before: client sparse BM25" %}
```sql
QUERY TEXT 'time travel' FROM books USING sparse LIMIT 10;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

`USING bm25` with no explicit model resolves to the same `qdrant/bm25`
sparse path.

## Step 1: index the payload field

Create a BM25-scored text index on the field the sparse vectors were built
from. Match the old encoder's tokenizer, language, stopwords, and stemmer so
rankings stay comparable:

{% qqlExample title="Add a BM25 text index" %}
```sql
CREATE INDEX ON COLLECTION books FOR title TYPE text
  WITH (scoring = 'bm25', tokenizer = 'word', lowercase = true);
```
{% /qqlExample %}

The client `qdrant/bm25` default is the `word` tokenizer with English
stopwords and stemming. If you changed `bm25_language`, `bm25_tokenizer`,
`bm25_stopwords`, or `bm25_stemmer`, set the equivalent index options.

## Step 2: switch the query

{% qqlExample title="After: server BM25" %}
```sql
QUERY BM25 'time travel' FROM books USING title LIMIT 10;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

`USING` changes meaning here: `sparse` was a vector name, `title` is a
payload field.

## Hybrid queries

Only the lexical leg changes. The dense leg keeps embedding with
`QUERY TEXT`:

{% qqlExample title="Before: dense + client sparse" %}
```sql
WITH
  dense AS (QUERY TEXT 'time travel' USING dense LIMIT 50),
  lexical AS (QUERY TEXT 'time travel' USING sparse LIMIT 50)
QUERY FUSION RRF FROM books PREFETCH (dense, lexical) LIMIT 10;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

{% qqlExample title="After: dense + server BM25" %}
```sql
WITH
  dense AS (QUERY TEXT 'time travel' USING dense LIMIT 50),
  lexical AS (QUERY BM25 'time travel' USING title PARAMS (k = 1.2, b = 0.75) LIMIT 50)
QUERY FUSION RRF FROM books PREFETCH (dense, lexical) LIMIT 10;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

## What to drop

For migrated legs, the client BM25 knobs no longer apply:

| Client sparse knob | Server BM25 equivalent |
|---|---|
| `bm25_k1` / `bm25_b`, `setBm25Params(k1, b, …)` | `PARAMS (k = …, b = …)` on the query |
| `bm25_avg_len`, `estimate_bm25_avg_len` | None — the server tracks document lengths |
| `bm25_language`, `bm25_tokenizer`, stopwords, stemmer | `CREATE INDEX … WITH (…)` options |

Keep the client options only if some legs still query the sparse vector or
the same code also runs on edge. Once nothing queries the sparse vector, you
can stop writing it on upsert.

## When not to migrate yet

- **Edge.** qdrant-edge has no server BM25; `QUERY BM25` fails with a typed
  error. Keep `USING sparse`.
- **Qdrant < 1.20.** Upgrade the server first; QQL rejects `QUERY BM25` with
  a version error instead of sending the request.
- **The sparse vector serves something else**, such as a learned sparse model
  (SPLADE, BGE-M3) or another consumer reading it directly.
- **The text is not in the payload.** Server BM25 can only score stored
  payload text; without it you would need a re-upload.
